WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:10.169
Now if we're going to talk about animation, obviously we need to think a little bit about how we're going to trigger that and very typically in the context of games it's going to be relating to input.

2
00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:13.169
So that's going to bring us on to our next subject.

3
00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:17.170
So input in Unreal is broadly broken up into two categories.

4
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:21.170
We have the old legacy system and we have the new enhanced input system.

5
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:27.170
Both of these can handle input from keyboards, mice, game pads, VR controllers.

6
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.170
So you can ultimately ship a game on either of these things.

7
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:40.170
The vast majority of online content is probably going to be talking about the legacy system because enhanced input is still relatively new.

8
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:48.170
It hasn't really been introduced since I think Unreal 5, but ultimately you can ship on either of these things.

9
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.579
So it's worth kind of looking into them both.

10
00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.579
So to begin with, in legacy input we have the two kind of key concepts.

11
00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:57.579
We have action mappings and axis mappings.

12
00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:04.579
Action mappings are your button presses, your mouse clicks, your kind of like this thing is on or it is off kind of inputs.

13
00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:19.579
Then we have axis mappings which relates to things like thumb sticks, it relates to mouse movement, anything that is kind of like continuous constant input that might be fractional and it's not on or off, it varies by degrees.

14
00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:26.579
All of these mappings can be edited and accessed through the developer settings, through the project settings menu.

15
00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:33.579
And if you're interested about like where any of these types are coming from, you can look at inputCoreTypes.h inside C++.

16
00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:44.579
Ultimately all of this input in this system gets filed through player input, which is an object that gets spawned by the player controller and that gets passed around to communicate with the various player input components,

17
00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:49.579
which will get added to your actors as they become enabled for input.

18
00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:57.420
So if we were to actually look at how we might implement these inputs, there's kind of two ways we can do it.

19
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.420
We can do it through code, which is much less common, but you can actually do this at runtime.

20
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.420
This is the default Pawn. You can actually see it adds its own custom inputs.

21
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:17.419
This is kind of helpful, I suppose, if you want to make sure that the project doesn't have to be set up correctly, as long as you've carried this code over, this character's or this Pawn's input is going to work.

22
00:02:17.419 --> 00:02:19.419
And then you have the more typical way of doing it through project settings.

23
00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:23.419
So if we were to quickly jump into our project, we can look at that.

24
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:30.419
Like I said, we go into edit, project settings, and then under the input section, we can see the inputs here.

25
00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:33.419
We have our action mappings, like we said, and our axis mappings.

26
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:38.419
And by default, when you start up most projects, it's going to have some pre-existing inputs.

27
00:02:38.419 --> 00:02:42.419
So clearly here, the jump input has already been made for us.

28
00:02:42.419 --> 00:02:46.419
We have these various look and move inputs under our axis mappings.

29
00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:52.509
And it's just worth briefly mentioning here that you can add as many of these as you like, but it's not exclusive.

30
00:02:52.509 --> 00:02:59.509
Just because spacebar is bound to our jump input doesn't mean we can't also have, I don't know, menu back.

31
00:02:59.509 --> 00:03:03.509
And we can also bind that to our keyboard spacebar.

32
00:03:03.509 --> 00:03:08.509
So these things, you do need to be a little bit careful about maintaining your own input settings

33
00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:14.509
and making sure that if you do have multiple actions that can have the same input,

34
00:03:14.509 --> 00:03:18.509
that you're kind of accounting for that so you're not getting weird crossover of logic there.

35
00:03:18.509 --> 00:03:26.550
Then we actually have the kind of using these inputs that we've made side of things,

36
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.550
and that's going to be the input binding.

37
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.550
This can be done in two ways. We can either do it through C++ or Blueprint.

38
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:37.550
The C++ way is definitely more verbose, but depending on the functionality you need,

39
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.550
they both provide the same, they expose the same functionality.

40
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.550
So typically here you have this set up player input component,

41
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.550
which is going to be part of the character flow.

42
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.550
And at this point, the input component gets passed in as a parameter.

43
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:56.550
And all you need to do is either bind actions or bind axes to the input component

44
00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.550
through these two function calls. These are very, very similar.

45
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.550
Essentially, all you do is you say the name of the action that you're trying to bind to.

46
00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:09.550
Then at the end, you provide the function this is going to be utilizing.

47
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:14.550
So in this case, these functions are going to take in slightly different things.

48
00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.550
For this jump function, it's just going to be a blank void function.

49
00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:23.550
But for this move forward function, it's going to need to take in a float parameter,

50
00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.550
which is your input for whatever axis this is bound to.

51
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:33.550
Both of them pass in this object, so what object this binding is going to be firing on.

52
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.550
But the key difference is that for the bind action,

53
00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.550
this is going to be firing based on this key event.

54
00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.550
So it's either going to be IE pressed or IE released,

55
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.550
depending on whether or not you've pressed that button or you want it to fire when it's released.

56
00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:50.550
Whereas for the bind axis, that's actually called every tick.

57
00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.550
So you're not binding that in a certain situation.

58
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.550
It's not when this input is over a certain amount.

59
00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.550
It's not doing deadzoning stuff.

60
00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.899
You would do that yourself in the function call.

61
00:05:02.899 --> 00:05:06.899
And then the kind of simpler implementation clearly is in Blueprints,

62
00:05:06.899 --> 00:05:08.899
where as soon as you've made an input,

63
00:05:08.899 --> 00:05:12.899
and you've compiled your Blueprints,

64
00:05:12.899 --> 00:05:14.899
these axes will actually be made available to you.

65
00:05:14.899 --> 00:05:19.899
So again, because we've already got jump and menu back as actions that we've made,

66
00:05:19.899 --> 00:05:22.899
if I were to just open up any old Blueprint,

67
00:05:22.899 --> 00:05:25.899
if I just make some actor,

68
00:05:25.899 --> 00:05:38.670
as soon as those inputs exist in my event graph, I can just bind them.

69
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:44.379
So input action jump comes up like that.

70
00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:48.379
Or indeed menu back is the new one I just made.

71
00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:50.379
You can see just it makes it so in that sense,

72
00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:53.379
it's a lot easier to do it through Blueprint instead of kind of a little bit less setup.

73
00:05:53.379 --> 00:05:58.269
But ultimately, it depends on what your needs for the project are.

74
00:05:58.269 --> 00:06:02.269
So the other system available to us, as we mentioned before, is enhanced inputs.

75
00:06:02.269 --> 00:06:05.269
This is primarily asset based. So it's not done through the project settings.

76
00:06:05.269 --> 00:06:09.269
This is actually all handled through assets in your content browser.

77
00:06:09.269 --> 00:06:11.269
And there's two key assets we need to be thinking about.

78
00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:16.269
We have input actions, which is actually the concept of something that you can do in your game.

79
00:06:16.269 --> 00:06:19.269
And we have context, which is really what marries all these things together.

80
00:06:19.269 --> 00:06:22.269
A context will define which actions apply in this context,

81
00:06:22.269 --> 00:06:26.430
and which buttons tie to each of those actions.

82
00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.430
The powerful thing about the enhanced input system

83
00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.430
is that all of this stuff can be easily easily chopped and changed at runtime.

84
00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.430
If you imagine a game where you're running around on foot,

85
00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:38.430
you're going to be worrying about different inputs,

86
00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.430
like picking things up, reloading your gun, shooting.

87
00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.430
Whereas when you jump into a car,

88
00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:45.430
you might actually need to listen for different buttons

89
00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.430
and you want responding even to the same inputs in different ways.

90
00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:52.430
So each of those things, you want to handle that in a dynamic way,

91
00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.430
and enhanced input can handle a lot of that stuff for you.

92
00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.519
If we were to actually go through the process of really briefly demonstrating

93
00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:03.519
the kind of relevant things when utilizing these assets.

94
00:07:03.519 --> 00:07:06.519
First off, they can be created in the content browser.

95
00:07:06.519 --> 00:07:13.480
You right-click, go to input, and you've got, not interchange, input.

96
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.480
And you have input action here.

97
00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.480
Typical naming convention will be IA.

98
00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:28.370
So if we do IA fire gun, and we'll just create another one for IA move.

99
00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:34.579
And then if we're going to create a context,

100
00:07:34.579 --> 00:07:38.579
at the same place, input, input mapping context,

101
00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:40.579
and typical naming convention there is IMC,

102
00:07:40.579 --> 00:07:43.930
and we'll just call it walk shoot.

103
00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.930
So inside an input action,

104
00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.930
there's actually not a huge amount of detail here,

105
00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.930
a little bit of default information, some description stuff.

106
00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.930
The key thing that we need to be worrying about

107
00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.930
is the value type of our action.

108
00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:58.930
So for a digital action is a little bit like our actions

109
00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.930
for our previous system, where it's kind of on or off.

110
00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:05.930
So that might be like firing the gun or not firing the gun.

111
00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.930
But we also have available three kinds of axes.

112
00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:09.930
So if you think about this,

113
00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.930
it's like this might be something like a trigger,

114
00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.930
one dimensional, it's only going from zero to one,

115
00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.930
or minus one to whatever.

116
00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.930
2D axis might represent something like your whole thumb stick.

117
00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.930
So it's your up and down and your left and right,

118
00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:26.930
or your mouse would be another 2D axis.

119
00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:30.930
And 3D axis is more typically going to be used by things like VR,

120
00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.929
but maybe also your mobile phone's gyroscope

121
00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.929
if you're doing a mobile game.

122
00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:36.929
So that's the key thing you need to be worrying about

123
00:08:36.929 --> 00:08:37.929
when defining an input action.

124
00:08:37.929 --> 00:08:40.929
So for example, in this case, fire gun would be digital,

125
00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:42.929
and our move,

126
00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:45.929
well, if it's meant to represent our whole 2D movement plane,

127
00:08:45.929 --> 00:08:49.929
then we would have that as a 2D vector.

128
00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:53.090
It's actually our mapping context then that, like we said,

129
00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.090
marries the inputs to those actions

130
00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:59.090
and can define which input actions do we care about at a certain time.

131
00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.090
So if we open up the input mapping context,

132
00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.090
we've got this list of mappings,

133
00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:07.090
and here you would choose the actions that you defined.

134
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:08.090
So again, you don't have to use all of them.

135
00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.090
It depends on what you're actually doing.

136
00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.090
It depends on the context, no pun intended.

137
00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:18.090
But here we'll just say we want to use our fire gun and our IA move.

138
00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.279
As you can see,

139
00:09:19.279 --> 00:09:21.279
even though these are different types of actions,

140
00:09:21.279 --> 00:09:24.279
ultimately the options available to us are the same.

141
00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:28.279
And the things we need to think about here are which buttons tie to this action.

142
00:09:28.279 --> 00:09:31.279
So in this case, I'll say it's our left mouse button,

143
00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:34.279
and for move, I'll just bind W for now.

144
00:09:34.279 --> 00:09:36.279
And then we have triggers,

145
00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:41.279
which essentially affect how this input fires this logic.

146
00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:45.279
So this is really useful for anyone who's ever had to think about making inputs.

147
00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:47.279
I only want to be able to quit out of this menu

148
00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:49.279
when you press and hold for a second.

149
00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:50.279
That's already handled for you here.

150
00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:52.279
There's a hold and release.

151
00:09:52.279 --> 00:09:55.279
There's hold, there's combo buttons, there's corded actions.

152
00:09:55.279 --> 00:09:57.279
All of those things are here for you,

153
00:09:57.279 --> 00:10:00.279
and it's handled at the input system level.

154
00:10:00.279 --> 00:10:03.019
And then the other thing we have is modifiers,

155
00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:06.019
which is useful for a whole bunch of different things.

156
00:10:06.019 --> 00:10:08.019
Dead zones being the really obvious ones.

157
00:10:08.019 --> 00:10:09.019
If you have your thumb sticks

158
00:10:09.019 --> 00:10:11.019
and you just don't want to listen to that input

159
00:10:11.019 --> 00:10:13.019
unless they've pushed a certain amount in that direction.

160
00:10:13.019 --> 00:10:18.019
The power being that particularly if you have a 2D axis,

161
00:10:18.019 --> 00:10:21.019
that dead zone doesn't...

162
00:10:22.019 --> 00:10:24.019
It's very typical if you're doing something like controlling a cursor,

163
00:10:24.019 --> 00:10:26.019
you want to move that cursor

164
00:10:26.019 --> 00:10:29.019
when you've moved either of your axes

165
00:10:29.019 --> 00:10:31.019
in total a certain amount in a direction,

166
00:10:31.019 --> 00:10:34.019
rather than just listening to each axis separately.

167
00:10:34.019 --> 00:10:36.019
So the dead zone provides the ability

168
00:10:36.019 --> 00:10:38.019
to listen to each individual axis separately

169
00:10:38.019 --> 00:10:40.019
or all of it together.

170
00:10:40.019 --> 00:10:41.019
And then you have other things

171
00:10:41.019 --> 00:10:42.019
like the ability to negate

172
00:10:42.019 --> 00:10:45.019
so that I can make my S button affect,

173
00:10:45.019 --> 00:10:47.019
make me go backwards.

174
00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:50.019
I can make it so that my D key registers

175
00:10:50.019 --> 00:10:52.019
the Y axis instead and that kind of thing.

176
00:10:52.019 --> 00:10:54.019
So modifiers give you a lot of way

177
00:10:54.019 --> 00:10:56.019
to actually change the input

178
00:10:56.019 --> 00:10:58.019
once it's been triggered by your trigger behavior,

179
00:10:58.019 --> 00:10:59.019
whatever that is.

180
00:10:59.019 --> 00:11:03.200
Then when we actually come to the binding,

181
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.669
we've got a couple of other things to do.

182
00:11:06.669 --> 00:11:09.669
So a little bit like our input system

183
00:11:09.669 --> 00:11:11.669
for the legacy system,

184
00:11:11.669 --> 00:11:14.669
we can bind to it through C++.

185
00:11:14.669 --> 00:11:18.669
We can also bind to it through Blueprint,

186
00:11:18.669 --> 00:11:21.669
but there's a kind of key step that we need to do

187
00:11:21.669 --> 00:11:24.669
is we actually need to register that mapping context.

188
00:11:24.669 --> 00:11:26.669
As we said, the whole point of this system

189
00:11:26.669 --> 00:11:29.669
is that it can be adjusted and added to

190
00:11:29.669 --> 00:11:32.669
and pulled in and pulled out at runtime.

191
00:11:32.669 --> 00:11:36.090
So that's done through adding contexts.

192
00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.090
All of this is handled

193
00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:40.090
through the enhanced input local player subsystem.

194
00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:42.090
And you can see here,

195
00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:43.090
this is how we would do that.

196
00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.090
We would get hold of the player controller

197
00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.090
and we'd get the enhanced input local player subsystem

198
00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:52.090
and then it's through that that we would add our context in.

199
00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:55.090
And this is whatever context asset you already have.

200
00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:56.090
You can do the same thing in Blueprint,

201
00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:58.090
which I'll demonstrate in a second.

202
00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:01.090
And then separately from that in C++,

203
00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.090
like with our previous system,

204
00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.090
we would bind our actions.

205
00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.090
The difference being here that all of our actions

206
00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:10.090
are referred to as actions.

207
00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:13.090
And then we have this much broader range

208
00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:14.090
of trigger events here,

209
00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.090
whereas previously it was just IE pressed and IE released.

210
00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:21.090
Here we have a whole different range of different options.

211
00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:24.090
We have triggered, started, ongoing, canceled.

212
00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.090
And that's something that you should definitely

213
00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.090
look into in more detail if that's of interest to you.

214
00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:31.090
But just to quickly demonstrate these nodes

215
00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.090
on the Blueprint level,

216
00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:36.090
if we actually go inside our BP actor

217
00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:39.090
that we used for our previous demonstration,

218
00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.090
we can do the same thing,

219
00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:43.090
but we can listen to IA fire gun now.

220
00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.090
And that's very, very simple, right?

221
00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.090
This is how we actually do the binding.

222
00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.090
Again, quite a bit simpler here.

223
00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:52.090
But to demonstrate actually registering

224
00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:56.090
our input mapping contexts,

225
00:12:56.090 --> 00:13:03.210
we would just do enhanced input local player subsystem,

226
00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:07.370
and you would do add context,

227
00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:11.980
add mapping context and remove mapping context.

228
00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.980
So these are the kind of two key nodes

229
00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:15.980
that you need to be caring about here.

230
00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:21.980
Your inputs, even if you bind to a gun input,

231
00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:23.980
this is not going to fire if your player controller

232
00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.980
doesn't currently have a mapping context on it

233
00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:29.980
that has that action bound.

234
00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:31.980
So here, before you do that,

235
00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.980
the typical thing might be something like begin play

236
00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:34.980
or something like that.

237
00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:37.980
You would add a mapping context.

238
00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.980
In this case, it would be our IMC walk, shoot one.

239
00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:42.980
And then when you want to move out of that context,

240
00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.980
you don't want to listen to any of those inputs anymore.

241
00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:51.169
And then remove that context.

242
00:13:51.169 --> 00:13:54.169
So that about covers it for the actual system itself.

243
00:13:54.169 --> 00:13:56.169
But it's worth zooming out a little bit

244
00:13:56.169 --> 00:13:59.169
just to think about how input actually flows

245
00:13:59.169 --> 00:14:02.169
through the kind of unreal in total.

246
00:14:02.169 --> 00:14:03.169
It's worth bearing in mind,

247
00:14:03.169 --> 00:14:05.169
I think I've already touched on this a little bit.

248
00:14:05.169 --> 00:14:08.169
But the fact that typically when we think about input,

249
00:14:08.169 --> 00:14:10.169
we do tend to think about player controllers and pawns.

250
00:14:10.169 --> 00:14:13.169
But actually, input can be handled through

251
00:14:13.169 --> 00:14:15.169
really any actor in the game.

252
00:14:15.169 --> 00:14:18.169
It can be handled through the level script actor

253
00:14:18.169 --> 00:14:22.169
and really any actor can be made to listen to input

254
00:14:22.169 --> 00:14:25.169
through the enable input function.

255
00:14:25.169 --> 00:14:26.169
So it's worth thinking a little bit

256
00:14:26.169 --> 00:14:30.169
about how input moves through our stack really.

257
00:14:30.169 --> 00:14:34.330
So first off, we have the actual hardware input, right?

258
00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.330
We're pressing the button on our keyboard,

259
00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:37.330
we're clicking the button on our mouse,

260
00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.330
we're pushing a stick on our gamepad.

261
00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:42.330
And that will move into the actual player input

262
00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:46.330
where we kind of might process that input in some way,

263
00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:47.330
do certain things to it.

264
00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:50.330
You can create your own player input class

265
00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:53.330
if you need to have a little bit more control over that.

266
00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:58.330
And then we essentially flow through a series of actors, right?

267
00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:01.330
We have the kind of first and foremost thing

268
00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:06.330
is any actors that we've called the set input enabled function on.

269
00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.330
So you could do that again,

270
00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.330
say we just have a,

271
00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:13.330
you might see certain tutorials suggest the way to do this.

272
00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:16.330
It's not something I would necessarily recommend to use too much.

273
00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:21.970
But if we just had a BP sum switch,

274
00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.970
we can actually have that switch bind to some input action.

275
00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:27.970
So if we just say, listen to the jump event.

276
00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.970
And at some point in our game code,

277
00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.970
we can call enable input.

278
00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.970
And by enabling input and providing a player controller,

279
00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:36.970
this would then,

280
00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.970
this thing that we've just called enable input

281
00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.970
would go to the top of our input stack.

282
00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:43.970
That is the thing that we're going to check against first.

283
00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:47.970
And it's up to that thing to choose to either consume that input,

284
00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:49.970
which you can also define in blueprint,

285
00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:51.970
you can say when this input gets used,

286
00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:53.970
do we consume that input or not?

287
00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:57.970
And if it doesn't, then it would filter on downwards.

288
00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:01.159
And it's worth highlighting as well that

289
00:16:01.159 --> 00:16:04.159
because you can call enable input on several actors,

290
00:16:04.159 --> 00:16:07.159
the order in which it goes is the latest actor

291
00:16:07.159 --> 00:16:09.159
to have enable input called on it

292
00:16:09.159 --> 00:16:11.159
will be the first one in that stack.

293
00:16:11.159 --> 00:16:13.159
And it goes in top to bottom order in that sense.

294
00:16:13.159 --> 00:16:15.159
Then we move on to the player controller

295
00:16:15.159 --> 00:16:17.159
and the player controller has the same opportunity

296
00:16:17.159 --> 00:16:19.159
to handle that input, consume it.

297
00:16:19.159 --> 00:16:22.159
And after that, it will filter through to level blueprint

298
00:16:22.159 --> 00:16:25.159
and only then does it go to our pawn.

299
00:16:25.159 --> 00:16:26.159
So even though typically,

300
00:16:26.159 --> 00:16:30.159
pawn is going to be responsible for our input

301
00:16:30.159 --> 00:16:32.159
the majority of the time,

302
00:16:32.159 --> 00:16:34.159
that's kind of the intended system,

303
00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:36.159
we're actually going through several things

304
00:16:36.159 --> 00:16:38.159
that would take priority over that

305
00:16:38.159 --> 00:16:40.159
enabled input actors, then player controller,

306
00:16:40.159 --> 00:16:42.159
then our level blueprint.

307
00:16:42.159 --> 00:16:45.159
And then the pawn that we're currently possessing.

308
00:16:45.159 --> 00:16:48.159
And it's only after that that that then goes on

309
00:16:48.159 --> 00:16:49.159
and affects other game logic.
